  "format": "tikz",
  "fig_idx": 0,
  "fig_no": 1,
  "caption": "\\label{fig:summary}\nSummary of the main results and related results from the literature. $G$ is a signed digraph on $[n]$, and $f$ is a BN on $G$. $\\fp(f)$ is the number of fixed points of $f$. $\\att(f)$ is the number of asynchronous attractors of $f$, that is, the number of inclusion-minimal set $A\\subseteq\\B^n$ such that $f^i(A)\\subseteq A$ for every $i\\in [n]$. We have $\\att(f)\\geq 1$ and $\\att(f)\\geq \\fp(f)$ since $x$ is a fixed point of $f$ if and only if $\\{x\\}$ is an asynchronous attractor of $f$. $f$ is unstable if, for all $i\\in [n]$ and $x\\in\\B^n$, we have $f^w(x)_i\\neq x_i$ for some word~$w$ (conclusion of Lemma~\\ref{lem:flipping_lemma}). $\\fix(f)$ is the minimum length of a word $w$ over $[n]$ such that $f^w(x)$ is a fixed point of $f$ for any $x\\in\\B^n$; if no such word exists then $\\fix(f)=\\infty$. $\\synch(f)$ is the minimum length of a synchronizing word for $f$; if no such word exists then $\\synch(f)=\\infty$. \n"
 },
